# Graphify

When the user types `/graphify`, invoke this skill. Graphify is a CLI tool
(`graphify`) that builds a local knowledge graph of your project.

## Available commands

| Command | What it does | CLI equivalent |
|---------|-------------|----------------|
| `/graphify` | Build knowledge graph on current directory | `graphify .` |
| `/graphify <folder>` | Build graph on a specific folder | `graphify <folder>` |
| `/graphify query "question"` | Ask a question against the graph | `graphify query "question"` |
| `/graphify path "A" "B"` | Find relationship between two concepts | `graphify path "A" "B"` |
| `/graphify explain "concept"` | Explain a focused concept | `graphify explain "concept"` |
| `/graphify update` | Incrementally update the graph (AST-only) | `graphify update .` |
| `/graphify watch` | Auto-sync graph as files change | `graphify . --watch` |
| `/graphify wiki` | Build agent-crawlable wiki | `graphify . --wiki` |

## Rules

- **Always prefer the graph over raw files.** When `graphify-out/graph.json` exists, run `graphify query` before grepping or reading source files for codebase questions.
- Use `graphify path "<A>" "<B>"` for relationships and `graphify explain "<concept>"` for focused concepts.
- Read raw files only for lines the graph or an earlier read already located.
- After modifying code, run `graphify update .` to keep the graph current (AST-only, no API cost).
- Dirty graph files are expected after updates — not a reason to skip graphify.
- If `graphify-out/wiki/index.md` exists, use it for broad navigation instead of raw source browsing.

## Execution

When the user types `/graphify <args>`, run the corresponding `graphify` CLI command in the project directory and report the results back to the user. Do not ask for confirmation — just run it.
